 I'd be happy to help you with your Wordpress CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) issue in the context of Next.js! CORS errors occur when a web application tries to make requests to a different domain, protocol, or port than its own and the server doesn't allow it due to security reasons. In this case, we'll focus on setting up CORS for a custom Wordpress REST API endpoint from a Next.js application.

First, let's make sure that your Wordpress site has CORS enabled. You can do this by adding the following code snippet to your `wp-config.php` file:

```php
define('WP_REST_CORS', true);
add_filter('rest_pre_dispatch_header_add', function ($headers) {
    $allowed_origins = ['http://yournextjsapp.com', 'https://yournextjsapp.com']; // Add your Next.js app domains here
    $origin = get_sanitized_request_header('Origin');

    if (in_array($origin, $allowed_origins)) {
        $headers['Access-Control-Allow-Origin'] = $origin;
    }

    return $headers;
});
```

Replace `yournextjsapp.com` with the actual domain name of your Next.js application. Make sure to whitelist all necessary origins if you have multiple applications or development environments.

Now, let's configure your Next.js application to work with the Wordpress REST API. Install the `isomorphic-unfetch` package if you haven't already:

```sh
npm install isomorphic-unfetch
```

Create or modify an existing file named `api.js` in the `lib` folder of your Next.js project:

